@@s014 When they arrive
@@s015 As soon as the old exams are available, this page becomes the analysis of how the exam is made: types of questions, most frequent topics, step-by-step solutions.
@@s016 How to practise now
@@s017 Redo the homework exercises <b>without looking</b> at your solution, with the tests open (<code>scala-cli test . -w</code>).
@@s018 Respect the style constraints: no <code>var</code>, <code>return</code>, exceptions unless asked.
@@s019 Practise the open questions: explain <b>in English</b>, in 3–4 sentences, why a solution works.
@@s020 Exam-style questions
@@s021 Written for the atlas on the model of the course exercises: they are not official exams. Every solution was compiled with Scala 3.8.4.
@@s022 1 · Counting with a fold
@@s023 Implement, without explicit recursion, a function that counts the elements of a list that satisfy <code>p</code>.
@@s024 Show the solution
@@s025 <code>z = 0</code> (empty list: zero elements). The step adds 1 only if <code>p(a)</code>. <code>count(List(1,2,3,4,5))(_ % 2 == 1)</code> gives 3.
@@s026 2 · The last element, perhaps
@@s027 Implement <code>lastOption</code> with a fold, without pattern matching.
@@s028 Show the solution
@@s029 Each step discards the accumulator and keeps the current element: at the end the last one remains. The type <code>[Option[A]]</code> is needed because <code>None</code> alone would have type <code>None.type</code>. Results: <code>Some(9)</code> for <code>List(7, 8, 9)</code>, <code>None</code> for the empty list.
@@s030 3 · Filtering and transforming together
@@s031 Given a function that can fail, keep only the successful results.
@@s032 Show the solution
@@s033 <code>foldRight</code> keeps the order. If <code>f(a)</code> is <code>Some(b)</code>, <code>map</code> puts it in front of the rest; if it is <code>None</code>, <code>getOrElse</code> returns the rest unchanged.
@@s034 4 · Depth of a tree
@@s035 Using <code>fold</code> on the trees of week 3, compute the depth: a leaf has depth 0, a branch 1 + the depth of the deeper child.
@@s036 Show the solution
@@s037 <code>g</code> for the leaves: 0, ignoring the value. <code>f</code> for the branches: 1 plus the maximum of the two. <code>depth(Branch(Branch(Leaf(1), Leaf(5)), Leaf(3)))</code> gives 2.
@@s038 5 · forAll on Option without pattern matching
@@s039 Implement <code>forAll</code> (true if the Option is empty or if the value satisfies <code>p</code>) using only <code>map</code> and <code>getOrElse</code>.
@@s040 Show the solution
@@s041 <code>map(p)</code> turns <code>Some(a)</code> into <code>Some(p(a))</code>. On <code>None</code>, "for all elements" is vacuously true: default <code>true</code>.
@@s042 6 · What does it print?
@@s043 Show the solution
@@s044 <code>twice</code>: <code>x</code> is by-name and appears twice, so the block is run twice. <code>twiceLazy</code>: the <code>lazy val</code> computes it once and then remembers it.
@@s045 7 · An infinite list with unfold
@@s046 Implement <code>iterate</code>: the LazyList <code>a, f(a), f(f(a)), …</code>, using <code>unfold</code>.
@@s047 Show the solution
@@s048 The state is the current value: I emit it and the new state is <code>f(x)</code>. Never <code>None</code>: the list is infinite. <code>iterate(1)(_ * 3).take(4).toList</code> gives <code>List(1, 3, 9, 27)</code>.
@@s049 8 · Variance
@@s050 With <code>class Cat extends Animal</code>: does <code>val o: Option[Animal] = Some(Cat())</code> compile? And <code>val a: Array[Animal] = cats</code> if <code>cats: Array[Cat]</code>? Why?
@@s051 Show the solution
@@s052 The first <b>yes</b>: <code>Option[+A]</code> is covariant, so <code>Option[Cat] &lt;: Option[Animal]</code>. The second <b>no</b>: arrays are mutable and in Scala invariant (<i>Found: Array[Cat], Required: Array[Animal]</i>). If they were, you could write a Dog into an array of Cats.
@@s053 9 · Open question, in English
@@s054 Why is <code>foldLeft</code> on a strict <code>List</code> tail-recursive while <code>foldRight</code> is not? When would you still prefer <code>foldRight</code>?
@@s055 Show a model answer
@@s056 Model answer
@@s057 "In foldLeft the combining function is applied before the recursive call, so the call is the last thing the function does and the compiler can turn it into a loop with constant stack space. foldRight must first compute the result for the tail and only then apply f to the head, so each element leaves a pending call on the stack. I would still use foldRight when the result must preserve the order of the list, for example when building a list with Cons, and on lazy lists, where f may return without forcing the rest and the fold can stop early."
